@startsalt
'
' Wireframe — Settings view. Authoritative for THREE things only:
'   1. element inventory (which named controls exist)
'   2. containment and reading order (header / sidebar | main / status)
'   3. ordinal sequence (nav order)
'
' NOT authoritative for pixels, spacing, colour, typography, icons, density or
' state styling. Salt draws monochrome boxes; the real UI is a dense workbench
' with two themes. Never diff a screenshot against this render.
'
' Drawn in the DESKTOP install state, which is the one with the most controls.
' The web build drops [Install okff] / [Remove] and shows the desktop-only note
' in their place — see the runtime-split table in ui-settings.md.
'
' Render: npm run wireframes
'
{
  {* <&git-branch> OKFForge | "  Search concepts, types, tags...  " | [Preview] | [Markdown] | [Split] | [theme] | [Learn] | [Open] | [Classify] | [Save] }
  {
    {
      <b>NAV
      [Learn OKF]
      [Explorer]
      [Editor]
      [Graph & Search]
      [Classify]
      [DeepAgents]
      [Plugins & MCP]
      [<b>Settings]
      --
      <b>SAMPLE-OKF | valid
      "  Filter files...  "
      --
      {T
      + agents
      ++ graph-engineer.md
      ++ index.md
      + knowledge
      ++ index.md
      }
    } |
    {
        <b>Settings
        "Theme and zoom live in the header."
        ==
        <b>Command line
        "Install okff to open a folder from any terminal."
        ' Salt eats a literal ~ as markup, so the second line reads <dir>
        ' rather than the real ~/my-okf. Copy is not part of the contract.
        {#
          okff .
          okff <dir>
          okff --help
        }
        "Installed at /usr/local/bin/okff"
        {
          [Install okff] | [Remove]
        }
        "Each okff opens its own window; windows share preferences."
      }
  }
  {- 22 concepts - 83 edges - healthy | agents/graph-engineer.md }
}
@endsalt
